{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"1. **SHOULD-FIX — The claimed server-boundary secret test never invokes a `createServerFn`.** [src/server/__tests__/app-config.golden.ts:380](/home/user/Developer/worktrees/settings-ui-env-to-db-260731/src/server/__tests__/app-config.golden.ts:380) serializes the pure `settingsStatusFromRows()` result. That proves the helper shape is sanitized, but not that `getAppSettingsStatusFn` or `saveAppSettingsFn` cannot accidentally serialize secret-bearing data after future handler changes. This leaves earlier finding #8 only partially implemented. Exercise both actual server-function boundaries with planted DB/env secrets and recursively scan their serialized responses.\n\n2. **SHOULD-FIX — The “concurrent disjoint updates” regression test is sequential.** [src/server/__tests__/app-config.golden.ts:512](/home/user/Developer/worktrees/settings-ui-env-to-db-260731/src/server/__tests__/app-config.golden.ts:512) describes two-tab safety, but the setup awaits one `applySettingsPatch` before starting the next. It verifies field-level patching, not concurrency. Run disjoint patches concurrently from separate DB clients/processes so SQLite locking and last-writer behavior are actually exercised.\n\n3. **SHOULD-FIX — Atomic rollback is not tested against a failure inside `db.batch`.** [src/server/__tests__/app-config.golden.ts:520](/home/user/Developer/worktrees/settings-ui-env-to-db-260731/src/server/__tests__/app-config.golden.ts:520) uses an unknown key, which `assertWritableKey` rejects before the batch is constructed. It therefore cannot prove that `applySettingsPatch` rolls back earlier statements when a later SQL statement fails. Induce a real constraint/SQL failure in a later batch statement and assert that no preceding write commits.\n\n4. **NIT — Configuration-status codes are computed and returned but never rendered.** [src/server/app-config.ts:470](/home/user/Developer/worktrees/settings-ui-env-to-db-260731/src/server/app-config.ts:470) returns `features.text/image/tts`, including the requested deterministic `missing-key` distinction, but [src/app/parents/reglages.tsx:50](/home/user/Developer/worktrees/settings-ui-env-to-db-260731/src/app/parents/reglages.tsx:50) never reads `status.features`. The page still communicates individual key state, so this is not broken UX, but the unused API surface does not fully deliver earlier finding #12’s feature-level status design.\n\n**Verdict: FIX-FIRST.** The runtime implementation itself looks sound: boot validation is infrastructure-only; DB-wins/env-fallback and four-operation secret semantics are correct; snapshots are threaded through provider operations; branding follows frozen story language; provider modules have no residual configuration reads from `serverEnv`; i18n parity and calm scans pass; goldens remain intact; `db.batch` is used correctly; and the Docker ARG-to-ENV fallback is coherent. Type-check, lint, and the complete golden lattice pass. Before shipping, strengthen the three tests above so the security, concurrency, and rollback guarantees are proven at the boundaries actually relied upon."}}
